      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:eaf"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-eaf-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-eaf-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 7 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 amount. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the Basic Service Charge and Excess Reactive Demand Prices, not on the ECCR multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on that amount, about 0.26 percent of those charges inside city limits, is not carried; nor is the franchise fee on the unpriced energy. (2) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Company may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.43 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.43 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Company does not meter for it. (3) Unpublished energy rates. On-Peak kWh take the hourly Real Time Pricing - Day Ahead price, which the Company makes available by 4:00 p.m. the day before (Saturday and Sunday prices on Friday), and Off-Peak kWh a customer-specific rate. The JSON-LD defines each as a PriceDefinition with no Price, so a modeled bill carries the Basic Service Charge, excess kVAR and their riders only, and the validator refuses it as :no-energy-ledger. (4) Fuel Cost Recovery. The On-Peak RTP-DA price recovers fuel on hourly incremental kWh at the hourly average marginal fuel cost, and \"the amount calculated at the off-peak rate will be increased under the provisions of the Company's effective FCR Schedules in the manner ordered by the Georgia Public Service Commission\", without naming one; neither TOU-FCR-7 nor TOU-FCR-TP-5 lists EAF. No fuel is priced. (5) Demand Side Management. The sheet names \"applicable Demand Side Management Schedule\" only in its Minimum Monthly Bill and has no DSM section, and DSM-C-15 applies to Commercial customers while EAF is for Industrial customers, so no DSM charge is carried. (6) Minimum Monthly Bill. The sheet sets it at the $245.03 Basic Service Charge plus ECCR, the applicable DSM schedule and the Municipal Franchise Fee, and no Price carries it. Every price here is positive, so a bill on these prices alone is never below it. (7) Qualifications and Term of Contract. The customer must be an Industrial customer on FPA operating an electric arc furnace of at least 50 MW monthly demand that it controls to near 0 kW in summer on-peak periods, stated in the eligibility rule's description; the term is the greater of the time remaining on its RTP contract or one year, renewed yearly unless terminated with 30 days' written notice. The Company is not responsible for a customer's failure to receive and act on the hourly prices. None is priced and none is carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
